    <if>
      <condition auto:"FAILED" type:"AND">
        <custom_item>
          type        : SQL_POLICY
          description : "max_connections imposes a server-wide connection ceiling within the approved bound"
          sql_request : "SELECT CASE WHEN @@global.max_connections BETWEEN 1 AND @MYSQL_MAX_CONNECTIONS@ THEN CONCAT('OK max_connections=', @@global.max_connections) ELSE CONCAT('NONCOMPLIANT max_connections=', @@global.max_connections) END"
          sql_types   : REGEX
          sql_expect  : "^OK "
        </custom_item>

        <custom_item>
          type        : SQL_POLICY
          description : "A per-user concurrent connection limit is in effect for all non-system accounts"
          sql_request : "SELECT CASE WHEN NOT @@global.max_user_connections = 0 THEN CONCAT('OK global max_user_connections=', @@global.max_user_connections) WHEN (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM mysql.user WHERE User NOT LIKE 'mysql.%' AND NOT User = 'root' AND max_user_connections = 0) = 0 THEN 'OK per-account max_user_connections set' ELSE CONCAT('NO_LIMIT ', IFNULL((SELECT GROUP_CONCAT(CONCAT(User,'@',Host)) FROM mysql.user WHERE User NOT LIKE 'mysql.%' AND NOT User = 'root' AND max_user_connections = 0),'NONE')) END"
          sql_types   : REGEX
          sql_expect  : "^OK "
        </custom_item>
      </condition>

      <then>
        <report type:"PASSED">
          description : "8.3 Set Maximum Connection Limits for Server and per User"
          info        : "Limiting concurrent connections to a MySQL server can be used to reduce risk of Denial of Service (DoS) attacks performed by exhausting connection resources.

Limiting the number of concurrent sessions at the server and per user level helps to reduce the risk of DoS attacks. MySQL provides mechanisms to limit the number of simultaneous connections that can be made at the server level or by any given account."
          solution    : "Connect to the MySQL Database as an administrator.

For example, to set the global default per user to 50 run the command:

SET PERSIST max_user_connections=50;

To control the maximum number of clients the server permits to connect simultaneously, set the max_connections system variable:

SET PERSIST max_connections=1000;

Additionally, MAX_USER_CONNECTIONS can be set per user as well as for a given period of time using CREATE or ALTER.

For example:

ALTER USER 'fred'@'localhost'
WITH MAX_CONNECTIONS_PER_HOUR 5
MAX_USER_CONNECTIONS 2;"
